        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">They do opposite chemistry and you need both.</strong> The cleaner is alkaline and removes grey, dirt and mildew. The brightener is acidic, removes nothing except iron and tannin marks, and exists to undo what the cleaner did to the wood's pH, colour and absorption.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Skipping the brightener is the most common preparation mistake there is, and it shows up as blotchy stain, muddy cedar, black rings around every screw, and a finish that needs redoing sooner. It costs $25 and fifteen minutes on a deck you are already working on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Apply it to a deck still wet from rinsing the cleaner, leave it ten to fifteen minutes without scrubbing, rinse thoroughly, then give the wood 24 to 48 hours and a water-drop test before any stain goes near it.
          </p>
